WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.290
Speaker 1: David, do you think there should
be a maximum age for president, like there

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.100
is a minimum age for president?

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.210
So let's ignore constitutionality, because
there simply you know, there's a question

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.250
as to whether you would be able to put in
a maximum age of let's imagine you could if

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.259
you wanted to, and forget the constitutionality
of it.

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:19.960
So there's there's really two questions here.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.419
There's two ways we could be thinking of a
maximum age.

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:29.320
One is we want to have some way of limiting
how long people can be in elected office because

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.909
we don't want career politicians.

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:34.200
So as an alternative to a term limit, you
could do a maximum age.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.230
That's one reason you might want to do it.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.770
The other side is because you genuinely think
we just shouldn't have people over a certain

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.720
age doing the job, even if they're not career
politicians either, because you think that

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.750
after a certain age people are less effective,
less cognitively able, or for whatever other

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:55.060
reason, you just don't want older people being
president or in some other position.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.780
So if the idea is the former, which is we
just want to restrict the number of years

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.550
someone can be in elected office to event
to prevent people like Chuck Grassley, Nancy

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.920
Pelosi from staying in power for so long,
they're sort of our other ways to deal with

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.040
that.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.300
Term limits is a far simpler one.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:20.240
Fixing gerrymandering to eliminating so many
safe seats where once you become the incumbent,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.670
no one from your party or another party is
going to knock you out.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.860
I think that would would reduce how long people
stay in power.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:35.570
There are some ideas that having a sort of
representative system rather than just individual

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.460
first past the post, it's either one winner
or sort of like a percentage based system

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.530
like they have in other countries would be
a way to do it.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.180
I'm not so sure about that.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.630
But if the idea is just to kind of go the
direction of preventing career politicians,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.940
I think there are simpler ways than a maximum
age and probably more palatable to the public

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:53.940
ways.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:02:00.250
Now, if your perspective is old people in
politics are just bad, remember that if the

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:05.741
general public overall decided that it's a
priority not to have older people win, they

00:02:05.741 --> 00:02:11.580
could say, well, we're going to vote for younger
people and you don't necessarily have to restrict

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.280
choice there.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.300
And then you get to the question of, well,
then why do we have a lower limit?

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.300
Right.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.110
Like if the American public genuinely thought
a 12 year old would be better than the candidates

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:24.340
that are adults, then they should vote for
that person.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:29.420
But you might say most Americans would realize
that that doesn't make sense.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.230
But then you remember that they did elect
Trump after all.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:36.709
So then that makes you question anything that
relies on the presumed intelligence of the

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:38.000
electorate.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.780
So I think that if you start going with an
upper limit and saying, well, you should have

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.570
an upper limit, that raises questions of why
do we have limits at all if it's really just

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.640
about letting the American people decide on
the best candidates?

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.239
I think it's sort of tough along those lines.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:58.120
The bigger question to me, if the aspect of
this that's most concerning is a cognitive

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.629
issue, is the possibility of saying here we're
going to put in place three or four different

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:09.040
requirements, that would include if you want
to have something like the Montreal cognitive

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.930
assessment, you have it, but maybe something
else as well.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.420
I'm not against it in principle.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.650
I think that it does get back to the issue
of constitutionality and pragmatics because

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.690
it'll just never happen.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:25.819
I mean, I would like to have a president imagine
for a second, and this is completely anti

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:26.819
constitutional.

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:30.959
It's in a sense it's authoritarian because
you're imposing limits that that could be

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:35.170
even misused in some way against a certain
sectors of the population.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:40.700
But imagine if to be a major, you know, to
be a party nominee or whatever, Democrats

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.850
and Republicans, and why would they do it?

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.710
Or the Constitution put in place a rule which
said you've got to do a Montreal cognitive

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.660
assessment, you need to do a psych eval, the
results of which will be published publicly.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.940
And if there's personality disorders or whatever,
it's going to be there.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.350
You have to do a basic on the spot like basic
American history and constitutionality, not

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.830
to the point of like you have the knowledge
of a constitutional lawyer, but just really

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.820
basic things about history.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:13.910
Who who were the allies and the axis of evil
in World War two or, you know, just really

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.720
simple things.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.720
Many candidates would immediately be humiliated
or they would actually have to study and take

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.680
it seriously and we would end up with more
informed candidates.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.560
That's super interesting to me, although I
think there's an authoritarian aspect to it,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.940
and I don't think there's any way that it
happens constitutionally.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.960
So the answer to should there be an upper
age limit my.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.870
Question to you is, what is the goal, is it
to prevent career politicians, is it to ensure

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.640
that our elected officials are cognitively
capable?

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.210
Is it because you just don't think, for whatever
reason, that the country should be led by

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.650
people above a certain age?

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:51.370
Depending on your answers to those questions,
the approach that could be taken varies pretty

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.370
dramatically.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.180
And I'd be interested in hearing from you
about that

